WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:18.830
Martha Bayles: I too have a slight cold. I
warn you. I don't know if there's a cough

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:32.220
button on this thing. I'll try to find it.
Thank you to Larry Arnn and to Tim Caspar,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:40.320
to Doug Jeffrey and Matt Bell and Mary Jo
Von Ewegen and also Kim Ellsworth, who helped

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:47.760
me negotiate my departure from snowy Boston
yesterday. Ninety-five inches of snow and

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:58.589
counting. I'm a New Englander. I just think
it's great. This is not just a cold and wintry

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:07.119
season in America. It's also an election season
coming up. I get that feeling from some of

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:13.500
the speakers. I'd like to start with that.
If you ask yourself what is the most important

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.540
thing about the upcoming American election,
I'm sure everyone here will have their answers.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:28.980
Many other Americans will have other answers.
The answer is different outside this country.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:35.980
Ninety-five percent of the people in the world
are not Americans. To those people, for billions

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.100
of them, billions of them, the most important
thing about the American election is that

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:50.300
the winning party is not going to round up
the losing party and put them in jail, however

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:59.250
much they might want to.
I gave a talk in China before the last Presidential

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:07.870
election at a Chinese university. I think
it was Fudan University in Shanghai. One of

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:16.430
the students asked who was going to be the
next American President. I said that I didn't

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:22.870
know. I could tell from their faces, or at
least I thought I could, and from some comments

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:29.170
I got afterward that they just figured I wasn't
a very important person because if I was an

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:36.090
important person, I would know who was going
to be the next President. I picked up on that

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:45.850
and I said, "Nobody knows."
They were incredulous. I felt obliged to explain

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:52.290
that a little bit, that it actually was a
contest, and that the winning party was not

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:58.390
going to put the losing party in jail. I could
see the smile on the face of my host, who

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:03.520
was a very experienced and a very wise foreign
service officer, who was very happy to see

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.069
this slipped into a lecture that was actually
supposed to be about something else.

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:17.069
This message is not really getting out there
these days, these kinds of messages. When

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:22.260
95 percent of humanity, well make that 90
percent because I think there's probably another

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:28.810
5 who do know America pretty well and do understand
these things. Maybe even 10 percent. Let's

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:35.810
say 85 to 90 percent of the people in the
world, when they look at America and its elections

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:44.970
and other things, what do they see? I'll give
you a little taste of what a lot of them see.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.060
This is true everywhere in the world, including
Europe.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:56.599
In early 2001, the State Department did one
of its rare follow-up studies of international

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:01.930
visitors. They have a fairly robust international
visitors program but they rarely do follow-up

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.030
studies, which is sort of odd, but that's
the way people do evaluation in Washington

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:13.030
these days. They commissioned a follow-up
study of 60 Mexicans who had been brought

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:20.879
here in an international visitor's program
in late 2000, early 2001.

00:04:20.879 --> 00:04:27.650
Here is part of the report's summary of what
the visitors had to say about the difference

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:34.460
between the Americans they met when they were
here and the Americans that they saw on their

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:42.719
television screens and movie screens. I quote,
"People who watch US television shows, attend

00:04:42.719 --> 00:04:48.759
Hollywood movies, and listen to pop music
can't help but believe that we are a nation

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:56.559
in which we have sex with strangers regularly.
We wander the streets well-armed and fully

00:04:56.569 --> 00:05:02.120
prepared to shoot our neighbors at the slightest
provocation and the lifestyle to which we

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:12.639
aspire is one of rich cocaine snorting." This
was before the release of Wolf of Wall Street.

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:18.610
The report continues, "This is not an accurate
depiction of the United States, nor is it

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:25.259
attractive to many people around the world.
The Mexican visitors were very clear that

00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:31.379
their images of America, shaped by commercial
entertainment media, were inaccurate and distorted

00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:38.379
and gave them a negative perception of the
United States." This was from one of our closest

00:05:38.469 --> 00:05:48.709
neighbors before 9/11. When they asked me
to set up an election debate, which they're

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:57.110
not going to do, this is a question I would
like to see debated in the upcoming election.

00:05:57.129 --> 00:06:01.759
This is a more general question but it relates
to the rest of my talk. I would like to see

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:11.399
the Democrats speak about what they think
government cannot and should not do. I'm sure

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:18.409
you all would like to hear that too. I would
also like to ask the Republican candidates

00:06:18.809 --> 00:06:25.240
what they think government can and should
do. It would be very interesting to hear if

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.080
people have to actually answer those questions.
Now the starting point of my book, Through

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:38.080
A Screen Darkly, and yes it's an illusion
to Paul, is that when the Cold War ended,

00:06:39.159 --> 00:06:45.339
the United States government got out of the
business of representing the United States

00:06:45.339 --> 00:06:53.589
to the rest of the world.
They allowed the job by default, by semi-conscious

00:06:53.589 --> 00:07:00.589
decision, to be taken over by the entertainment
industry. This was a decision that was made

00:07:00.589 --> 00:07:07.569
gradually over the '90's, culminating in the
termination of the USIA in 1999, that would

00:07:07.569 --> 00:07:13.929
be the US Information Agency, which was a
semi-autonomous agency that used to handle

00:07:13.929 --> 00:07:20.929
what is called public diplomacy around the
world. It was bi-partisan. It was made with

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:26.749
very little debate.
As one conservative veteran of USIA put it

00:07:26.749 --> 00:07:33.749
to me, he said, "That was a piece of bipartisan
bad judgment." My talk today will have three

00:07:34.429 --> 00:07:41.429
parts. The first is to look at the current
crisis in US public diplomacy and to say a

00:07:44.429 --> 00:07:50.409
little bit about how it developed over time
and why it happened the way it did, briefly,

00:07:50.409 --> 00:07:55.419
very briefly. Second to talk about the role
of exported popular culture; our Hollywood

00:07:55.419 --> 00:08:02.419
films, our TV shows, our popular music, in
deepening the crisis, the crisis being of

00:08:05.059 --> 00:08:12.059
America's reputation. Third to talk about
what would be required as a solution, or what

00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:21.039
would be required as part of a solution.
To talk about the first part, I would just

00:08:21.059 --> 00:08:26.800
briefly summarize what I have distilled as
the purposes of public diplomacy. The term

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:33.800
public diplomacy was coined in the early '60's
as a substitute for propaganda, which had

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:41.029
acquired a rather negative connotation during
the course of the 20th Century. I'll say a

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:46.400
little bit more about why it's different from
propaganda, which I strongly feel it's different

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:53.080
from propaganda. It has three main things
that public diplomacy does.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:09:00.080
The first is it reports the news in a responsible,
accurate, comprehensive, fair and thorough

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:06.850
way in parts of the world where the media
are strongly censored or strongly biased or

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:13.850
simply undeveloped. It provides news, especially
local and regional news, to parts of the world

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:20.840
where there's only a state service or there's
only a very propagandist local news. This

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:25.840
is the history of Radio Free Europe, Radio
Liberty, Radio of Free Asia, which began in

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:34.000
the '90's. Some other of our broadcast entities
claim to be doing this. It's not clear to

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:40.510
me they are.
That's a very important ... I think it's the

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:46.250
politically most acceptable right now function
of public diplomacy because as I'm sure you

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:53.250
know, other governments in the world, notably
Russia and China and others, and Iran and

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.820
others, are spending huge amounts of money
on not only broadcasting within their own

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:03.720
countries but broadcasting in the region,
in other languages, and around the world in

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:10.640
a kind of CNN style English language headline
news, Russia Today being the most prominent

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:20.000
example of it. Russia Today now broadcasts
in most of the world in English, Spanish,

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:27.020
Arabic, and one other language which is escaping
me at the moment. Oh, Mandarin. Mandarin.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:31.700
We of course do nothing very much like that
right now.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:38.700
Number two, to present what Harry Truman called
a full and fair picture of American society,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:44.900
history, culture and institutions. Here I
would also emphasize political institutions

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:54.580
and political traditions. This is really not
being done at all now. It's unfortunate. You

00:10:54.580 --> 00:11:00.230
might wonder whether the government is capable
of doing it. I think that's a very valid question.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:04.430
All I can say is it's not going to be done
by the commercial sector because there's really

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:11.430
not much money in it. The same goes for reporting
the news in Uzbekistan or in the mountain

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:16.420
region between Pakistan and Afghanistan. You're
not going to make a lot of money doing that.

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.380
You're not going to make a lot of money broadcasting
in a lot of places. Who's going to do it?

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:24.970
It's not going to be done by the commercial
sector.

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:33.410
Third is another one that's a big problematic
which is to advocate for the United States,

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:39.370
for its ideals, for its intentions in your
part of the world or the world in general,

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:45.760
and its interests. In other words, to explain
foreign policy. Of course you have to have

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:53.600
a foreign policy that can be explained. To
have the responsibility of explaining it to

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:58.010
the rest of the world and with the feedback
that comes with that, might actually be an

00:11:58.010 --> 00:12:04.290
added incentive to actually have a coherent
foreign policy. Who knows? The decline of

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:10.330
public diplomacy was, as I said, gradual.
It started actually before the end of the

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:16.070
Cold War, with the so-called Culture Wars
and the counter-culture of the '60's and the

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.590
Vietnam War.
With the divisions that arose in American

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:25.530
society, a lot of people who had formerly
been very helpful with the USIA and very willing

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:35.650
to go on tours and give lectures and be part
of that effort, academics, journalists, artists,

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:44.250
writers, went pretty far to the left for the
most part, became alienated from doing propaganda

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:51.250
for the government. There's many, many occasions
I could describe to you of this happening.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:58.670
Meanwhile, when President Reagan came in and
made the wonderful and colorful Charles Z.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:08.790
Wick head of USIA, there was a real push to
make US public diplomacy a really finely honed

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:15.430
Cold War weapon. That mattered. I know some
people who did that. It was good work and

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:22.430
they did it well for the most part. That,
in some ways, furthered the divisions.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:32.160
It was seen as propaganda by some of its critics
and sometimes it was. Then came the end of

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:40.120
the Cold War. USIA was, you might say, an
exhausted agency. It had really done a lot.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:47.050
It was not under great leadership. When Jesse
Helms decided to cut a lot of US government

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:50.800
agencies, including the National Endowment
for the Arts and all these other ones that

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:57.740
were really in his sights, the only one that
actually went down was the USIA. That happened

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:03.710
in 1999. Even before that, there had been
serious cuts in all of these programs, including

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:09.270
educational exchanges, cultural exchanges,
things that were seen as not directly relevant

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:14.520
to the ideological battle.
I would make the argument that those things

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:20.980
are crucial, even though they don't necessarily
move the needle of public opinion in foreign

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:26.370
countries in a way that you can discern over
a matter of weeks or months. They build up

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:33.370
connections and relationships and channels
of communication that are there in a crisis.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:40.600
Similarly with the news, it's a slow build-up
of trust. The people have to know that they're

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:47.360
going to get a reliable sense of what's happening
from the Voice of America or Radio Free Europe.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:52.490
You can't just drop in and say, "Hi. We're
here with the truthful news" because everybody's

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:59.080
doing that. You have to build up trust over
time. This is a long-term process.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:08.440
As I say, it looked like a Cold War relic
in the '90's. We needed a peace dividend and

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:17.550
so forth and so on so it got cut. It got cut
rather drastically. At the same time in the

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:23.770
'90's, huge changes in the world. Huge changes
in what would be the practice of this thing.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:30.110
Very briefly, the first was in media. Even
before the internet, you had the tape cassette.

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:35.710
You had satellite TV, a medium whose importance
is often overlooked in this discussion.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:41.820
It was satellite TV that penetrated the entire
Middle East, that penetrated huge swaths of

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:47.910
the world that had never seen anything but
their local state TV. A great bulk of it was

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:53.779
entertainment. I'll get to that in a minute.
Then of course came the internet and our beloved

00:15:53.779 --> 00:16:02.259
social media. New players, the rise of NGOs
in the '90's was astronomical. Businesses

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:09.860
became more and more involved overseas. Churches
expanded missionary efforts and more short

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:16.490
term kinds of involvement overseas. Universities,
needless to say, have expanded.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:23.490
Just to sum it up, there's a lot of non-governmental
players who are involved. Some people say,

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.350
"Well that is our public diplomacy. This is
America. We don't want the government doing

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:33.750
it. We want all these private sector institutions
and organizations doing it." I think that's

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:38.660
a very cogent argument. I actually went along
with that for a while. When you look at it

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:44.630
from the point of view of other countries,
there's an expectation that the US government

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:49.160
is going to speak and that the US government
will have a voice and it will have a voice

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:56.160
and it will talk to you in Oman or in some
other country, that the US government had

00:16:56.470 --> 00:17:02.440
something to say and invites you to listen.
If that government voice isn't there ... I

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:08.529
traveled to 11 countries researching my book.
I must have talked to over 300 individuals

00:17:08.529 --> 00:17:15.039
overseas from all different walks of life.
The most consistent complaint was really that

00:17:15.039 --> 00:17:20.309
Americans don't pay attention. "They don't
talk to us. They don't listen to us. They

00:17:20.309 --> 00:17:25.449
don't care about us." In a world that's supposed
to be anti-American, that's a rather striking

00:17:25.449 --> 00:17:32.449
sentiment. I've heard it reflected by many
others. Then of course we had new adversaries,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:38.320
non-state actors.
Islamic state I would argue is neither Islamic

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:46.120
nor a state. It's certainly not a country.
You may call it totalitarian but it's not

00:17:46.139 --> 00:17:55.299
totalitarian in any 20th Century sense because
it's a bunch of roving gangsters, who are

00:17:55.299 --> 00:18:01.249
turning upon each other as much upon their
presumed enemies. The Middle East is in a

00:18:01.249 --> 00:18:09.769
state of violent and chaotic religious wars.
There's lots of non-state actors there, who

00:18:09.779 --> 00:18:14.649
can get their hands on weapons, as was pointed
out earlier this morning. Then you have the

00:18:14.649 --> 00:18:21.649
new authoritarian regimes.
You have China and you have Russia and Iran

00:18:21.919 --> 00:18:28.700
and some others. They're different from the
Cold War because they deliver, at least China

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:34.619
does and Russia kind of did, they deliver
prosperity to at least a very significant

00:18:34.619 --> 00:18:41.159
part of their population. As you know, the
Cold War was as much about prosperity, America's

00:18:41.159 --> 00:18:46.190
prosperity, as it was about our freedom and
our way of government. It was about all the

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:51.679
goodies that we had and our comfortable way
of life. It's not about that so much anymore.

00:18:51.679 --> 00:18:56.820
It's about something else because the Chinese
Model, as it's so-called, the Beijing Model.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:02.440
I know it's gotten a little rocky lately,
was that you could have an authoritarian government

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:08.619
and deliver prosperity and you can just administer
the population and control the population

00:19:08.619 --> 00:19:18.110
and you don't need any of these messy and
annoying democratic institutions. Give them

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:28.630
bread and circuses and deny them basic political
liberties. Speaking of circuses, the other

00:19:28.639 --> 00:19:35.350
thing that happened in the '90's was the explosive
spread of American popular culture, American

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:40.690
entertainment, commercial entertainment, throughout
the world. As these new technologies arose,

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:47.690
notably satellite television, they had to
fill their hours. What did they do? They bought

00:19:47.919 --> 00:19:57.799
the worse American soap operas, the Bold and
the Beautiful, all sorts of American programs,

00:19:57.809 --> 00:20:00.340
some good.
Don't get me wrong. I've spent most of my

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:04.279
adult life writing about popular culture.
I don't think it's all terrible by any means

00:20:04.279 --> 00:20:10.580
or I would have done that. It has changed
in many ways for the worse. I think it's on

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:17.580
a downward trajectory right now. The worst
stuff got out there in all sorts of place

00:20:17.779 --> 00:20:22.769
to all sorts of people, in a completely uncontrolled
way.

00:20:22.769 --> 00:20:29.720
It's even impossible to measure the full impact
and the full spread because even if you look

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:35.070
at the numbers, which I'll give you in a minute,
they only cover the legal export. Usually

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:40.419
just the legal export to the more lucrative
markets. That ain't the whole world and it

00:20:40.419 --> 00:20:48.819
doesn't cover the piracy, the illegal DVDs,
the knock off DVDs, which is a huge industry,

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:53.379
not to mention the internet and all the illegal
downloading that goes on. It's impossible

00:20:53.379 --> 00:20:59.960
to measure the spread of this.
I've arrived at my second topic, which is

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:06.679
the impact of our popular culture, our entertainment,
the perceptions of the United States and of

00:21:06.679 --> 00:21:15.039
Americans who we are as human beings. Now
one reason why this was allowed to happen

00:21:15.049 --> 00:21:20.330
was because of the history of the Cold War.
During the Cold War, American popular culture

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:29.830
was one of America's secret weapons. In fact,
several Russians said after the collapse of

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:35.009
the Soviet Union, many, many Russians testified
to having listened to jazz on the Voice of

00:21:35.009 --> 00:21:42.009
America or having later on a younger generation
listening to some rock music, selected rock

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:49.210
music and soul music on the Voice of America.
The power of that music either conveyed by

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:55.039
the governmental channel or just informally
as it just sifted into those countries, was

00:21:55.039 --> 00:22:02.039
smuggled in an so forth, was astounding. As
I'm sure a lot of you know, the so-called

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:11.029
Velvet Revolution in Czechoslovakia, one of
the heroic figures or the heroic figures from

00:22:11.029 --> 00:22:18.029
that period were a little rock band called
the Plastic People of the Universe. This is

00:22:18.029 --> 00:22:22.210
not a rock band you want to sit down and kick
back your heels and listen to their music.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:27.739
They were more like an absurdist performance
art, which is really not my thing. The thing

00:22:27.739 --> 00:22:31.860
about the Plastic People of the Universe in
Prague is they wouldn't stop doing what they

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:35.330
were doing.
The Communist authorities cracked down on

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:39.559
them every conceivable way. They wouldn't
stop doing what they were doing. The just

00:22:39.559 --> 00:22:44.809
kept doing what they were doing. After a while,
they attracted the attention of Vaclav Havel

00:22:44.809 --> 00:22:54.009
and of his associates, who are more serious
political actors. They became a cult hero.

00:22:54.009 --> 00:22:58.320
To this day, if you talk about that little
band in Prague, you will get a response from

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:05.320
people. That was true. We have an uncritical
attitude toward our popular culture at the

00:23:05.389 --> 00:23:09.820
end of the Cold War. It was something that
had really made a difference in parts of the

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:16.350
world and a difference for the good. Even
our counter-cultural rock music of the '60's,

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:23.790
which was regarded with loathing at home by
many people, was understood to be subversive

00:23:23.799 --> 00:23:29.039
in these foreign settings. You may not like
it here but over there it was pretty subversive,

00:23:29.039 --> 00:23:35.179
so it had to be good.
We didn't feel real critical about this. It

00:23:35.179 --> 00:23:40.830
was a laissez-faire. Just let it all go out
there. It will bring messages of freedom.

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:51.630
It will bring messages of democracy. I won't
say that it hasn't done that. I will say that

00:23:51.639 --> 00:24:00.039
it's carried a lot of other messages with
it along as well. In particular, that Americans

00:24:00.039 --> 00:24:08.759
have a taste for the extreme vulgarity, that
American relish extreme and hideous graphic

00:24:08.759 --> 00:24:14.509
violence, that we find this very amusing and
we love to watch it and that we are a very

00:24:14.509 --> 00:24:21.509
vitriolic people, quite hateful and spiteful
and cynical in our political discourse and

00:24:22.029 --> 00:24:29.029
indeed in all of our discourse.
This is all true of course but I use the metaphor

00:24:29.529 --> 00:24:39.159
of a fun-house mirror or maybe to update it,
a bad selfie. There's truth to it but it's

00:24:39.159 --> 00:24:48.279
grossly exaggerated. It's blown out of proportion.
The good parts of America, the part that offsets

00:24:48.279 --> 00:24:52.239
that, the part that after we're through watching
the violent movie we go out and walk down

00:24:52.239 --> 00:24:57.190
the street and we don't really think we're
going to get shot at, all that part, the normal

00:24:57.190 --> 00:25:07.350
part, the part that's us, airbrushed out.
Our religiosity, airbrushed out. Our civil

00:25:07.379 --> 00:25:13.769
society, our institutions, our voluntary organizations,
Hillsdale College, its supporters, people

00:25:13.769 --> 00:25:20.299
like you and me, airbrushed out. Not just
conservative people but decent had working

00:25:20.299 --> 00:25:27.299
Americans who try to hold their families together
who have certain basic values, airbrushed

00:25:27.859 --> 00:25:31.269
out.
Now again, not entirely, not always. I could

00:25:31.269 --> 00:25:37.299
give you a list of things that if I were a
culture, I won't say czar, because I think

00:25:37.299 --> 00:25:43.359
that words not go over well. If I were in
charge of cultural export, I might consider

00:25:43.359 --> 00:25:51.470
I have a list of things I'd like to broadcast.
I'm not sure that's the way to go. Okay well

00:25:51.470 --> 00:26:00.869
I won't ... This export of popular culture
expanded very, very drastically. The total

00:26:00.869 --> 00:26:10.269
export sales figure for the US entertainment
in 2012 was $35.6 billion. That was an increase

00:26:10.299 --> 00:26:22.409
about four-fold from the 1908's. That's the
legal distribution of American popular culture.

00:26:22.409 --> 00:26:27.289
Now what is the evidence? What evidence do
I have for making these claims, apart from

00:26:27.289 --> 00:26:33.169
all the people I talked to. The people I talked
to have influenced me, I will grant. They've

00:26:33.169 --> 00:26:40.169
influenced me a lot. There's also a little
bit of other kinds of evidence. The Pew World

00:26:40.889 --> 00:26:47.649
Attitudes Survey, which is a huge sample of
people, over 45,000 people in 47 countries.

00:26:47.649 --> 00:26:55.369
No, it's more than that. It's 300 something
thousand people in 47 countries. They do show

00:26:55.379 --> 00:26:59.859
that a majority of people in the world, at
least the people they polled, said they like

00:26:59.859 --> 00:27:05.070
American movies, music and TV. You can't deny
that. You wouldn't be making all that money

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:13.230
overseas of people didn't like this stuff.
Of course this is disliked in the entire Muslim

00:27:13.259 --> 00:27:19.850
majority world, which won't surprise anyone.
It's disliked in Russia. It's disliked in

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:26.850
India, where people are very pro-American.
I could talk about that a little bit. Here's

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:34.100
I think a more interesting finding from Pew.
They find majorities in a great many countries

00:27:34.279 --> 00:27:44.239
agreeing with the statement, "It's bad that
American ideas and customs are spreading here."

00:27:44.249 --> 00:27:50.100
This is in friendly countries, European countries,
lots of places. I just ask, Pew doesn't ask

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:56.669
this but I do, where do these people get their
impression of American ideas an customs? The

00:27:56.669 --> 00:28:08.229
answer is from our popular culture.
Now I start my book with an interview I did

00:28:08.249 --> 00:28:17.399
with not a terrorist but a very disruptive
and unpleasant Islamist in Jakarta, in Indonesia,

00:28:17.399 --> 00:28:22.129
who goes around with baseball bats breaking
the windows in movie theaters and things like

00:28:22.129 --> 00:28:27.440
that. They don't set off bombs but they do
very disruptive things. He spent some time

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:34.440
in Saudi Arabia and brought back a Jubbaiyahist
view of Islam that does not sit well in Indonesia,

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:40.590
where they have their own version of Islam
and they're a very multicultural society.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.499
They don't really cotton to this version of
it.

00:28:43.499 --> 00:28:50.259
By the way, there are more Muslims in Indonesia
than in all the Arab countries combined. This

00:28:50.259 --> 00:28:55.450
guy is pretty well known in Indonesia. I start
my book with an interview with him. He says

00:28:55.450 --> 00:29:03.649
what you'd expect. America is out to destroy
Islam and our culture is one of our weapons.

00:29:03.649 --> 00:29:11.289
He believed firmly, as do many ordinary people,
because their own media have always been owned

00:29:11.299 --> 00:29:18.869
by the state, they think that this stuff is
coming from the US government. Why wouldn't

00:29:18.869 --> 00:29:23.559
they? They don't understand that this is an
independent, privately owned industry in the

00:29:23.559 --> 00:29:28.470
United States. It's not true in their country.
It's not true in most countries. They think

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:42.070
the US government is sending them Sex in the
City, along with some other worse things.

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:49.100
You can respond to that guy by saying nothing's
going to change his mind. I think that's true.

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:55.149
Besides, it's the price we pay for freedom.
It's the price we pay for liberty so just

00:29:55.160 --> 00:30:04.440
get over it. I think that's the dominant attitude
today in America across the political spectrum.

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:08.899
After I talked to him, I talked to a woman
who works in television in Jakarta. She was

00:30:08.899 --> 00:30:14.409
a lovely lady. She was no radical. She was
a religious lady but she didn't cover her

00:30:14.409 --> 00:30:21.039
hair. She was middle class, spoke very good
English, just a regular person like you and

00:30:21.039 --> 00:30:29.719
me, very smart, very stylish.
She said she was really concerned about how

00:30:29.739 --> 00:30:35.649
Indonesian television programs were imitating
American programs and they were having a bad

00:30:35.649 --> 00:30:41.769
effect on her kids. It could have been an
American mother talking. It was no different.

00:30:41.769 --> 00:30:46.309
She talked about some show that was on during
dinnertime and it showed a woman being strangled

00:30:46.309 --> 00:30:51.279
to death on the screen. She said, "I don't
want my kids watching some woman getting strangled

00:30:51.279 --> 00:30:57.429
to death on the screen when they're having
dinner." I've heard, throughout my career,

00:30:57.429 --> 00:31:01.049
I've heard plenty of Americans express the
same sentiment.

00:31:01.049 --> 00:31:07.190
I began my book that way because I'm trying
to show that I'm not worried about what violent

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:13.419
extremists, radicalist, Jihaddist Muslims
think about our culture. I mean they would

00:31:13.419 --> 00:31:19.799
condemn Shakespeare as well. I'm worried about
what people like Rossini Salhala thinks, the

00:31:19.799 --> 00:31:25.580
lady that I talked to, because there's more
people like her in the world than there are

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:35.679
like Rasique Muhammed, the first man.
Unlike Americans, when these people see some

00:31:35.679 --> 00:31:41.649
of our worst material that we export, they
can't adjust the picture. They can't say,

00:31:41.649 --> 00:31:48.289
"Well that's Breaking Bad." Not everybody
in Albuquerque is into meth. Not every chemistry

00:31:48.289 --> 00:31:58.009
teacher is cooking meth. Not every family
in America is hoarding guns and dead bodies

00:31:58.009 --> 00:32:03.249
and all this horrific stuff that goes on in
these programs. Some of them very well-made

00:32:03.249 --> 00:32:08.099
programs, I must admit, and entertaining.
We can be entertained by them. We can laugh

00:32:08.099 --> 00:32:15.099
at it. We can be offended by it. We can be
perhaps even think that it's a serious show,

00:32:15.389 --> 00:32:19.149
that it's trying to make us think about something.
We go about our daily life. We know that's

00:32:19.149 --> 00:32:24.440
not really an accurate portrait of our society.
There are billions of people in the world

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:31.259
who cannot do that. They have no way of adjusting
the picture. Now even worse I think, now let

00:32:31.259 --> 00:32:35.929
me shift away a little bit, even more concerning
to me as I've thought about this over time,

00:32:35.929 --> 00:32:41.419
than the vulgarity and the violence and the
vitriol, is the portrait of American life

00:32:41.419 --> 00:32:48.830
and institutions, particularly our political
institutions, our business institutions, our

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:57.110
private sector, our daily life, our communities.
That to me is in some ways more disturbing

00:32:57.119 --> 00:33:01.739
because that really speaks ...
I think you can maybe assume the other stuff

00:33:01.739 --> 00:33:06.739
is a pandering to the lowest common denominator
and many people in other countries are smart

00:33:06.739 --> 00:33:12.950
enough to figure that out. The portrait of
our institutions really bothers me because

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:24.830
it fits all too well with the propaganda of
our enemies. If you hired a PR firm to portray

00:33:24.830 --> 00:33:30.609
American society and institutions and culture
in a way that would feed directly into Vladimir

00:33:30.609 --> 00:33:35.309
Putin's anti-American propaganda machine,
you could not do better than what most of

00:33:35.309 --> 00:33:41.139
what Hollywood is doing these days. There's
just no getting around that.

00:33:41.139 --> 00:33:47.009
In Germany, I was in Germany in October giving
a series of lectures. I met some young people,

00:33:47.009 --> 00:33:51.489
Germans, who are really into the series I
mentioned a minute ago, Breaking Bad. I expect

00:33:51.489 --> 00:33:57.649
there probably aren't too many Breaking Bad
fans in here. Maybe there are. Who knows?

00:33:57.649 --> 00:34:03.859
You can read my review in the Claremont Review
of Books. This young man, he was a thoughtful

00:34:03.859 --> 00:34:09.240
young man and he was working for one of the
political foundations in Berlin. I asked him,

00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:13.929
"Gee you like Breaking Bad. What do you and
your friends get out of that show?"

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:28.000
He said, "Well it's a portrait of how Americans
will do anything for money. It's a portrait

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:35.000
of the desperate straits of Americans and
the lawlessness of America and the drug problems

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:44.590
in America." I said, "we have those kinds
of things in America." You can imagine I argued

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:52.910
with him the way I'm talking to you. He took
it in. I don't know if I made a dent in him.

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:59.720
Another example, House of Cards. How many
of you have watched the American version of

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:08.880
House of Cards. Ah, see, see, okay. Quite
a few. I think it's pretty bad. You can also

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:14.330
read my review of that in the Claremont Review
of Books. It's a slick production. If you

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:18.840
ever spent any time in Washington you will
not recognize what it tries to make Washington

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:26.720
look like. Worst of all, it isn't about what's
wrong in Washington. It's about sort of a

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:31.560
Macbeth story, a conspiracy you know, which
worked really well in the British version

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:34.650
by the way. I recommend the British version
because it was about a struggle within the

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:40.310
conservative party for power. The Macbeth
thing kind of worked.

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:45.220
In the American version, the guy had spent
I think one internship in Washington, as opposed

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:48.670
to the guy who did the British version who
had spent 4 years working in the Thatcher

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:53.310
government or working for Margaret Thatcher
and in the conservative party. One knew a

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:59.710
lot about what he was dealing with, the other
knew practically nothing and it shows. Despite

00:35:59.710 --> 00:36:09.870
all this, the show is very popular online
in China, on the Sohu streaming website. Now

00:36:09.890 --> 00:36:15.900
when I say very popular, it had a few hundred
million viewers, which is a pittance in China.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:20.350
Among those viewers included several high
officials in the Chinese Communist Party,

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:29.430
who went around praising the show and recommending
it to their friends. Is this good new or bad?

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:33.750
The old Cold War thing is, "Well Chinese officials
are watching an American TV show. That must

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:39.130
be liberating, right?"
I'm currently sponsoring a Chinese Fulbright

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:45.160
Scholar at Boston College a man from Beijing
who teaches American studies and film at the

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:55.320
Chinese Foreign Studies University in Beijing.
He argues sometimes that every American cultural

00:36:55.330 --> 00:37:02.160
export, no matter how awful, is a double edged
sword, including House of Cards, because no

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:08.310
matter how negative the picture, what really
come across is American freedom of expression.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:12.960
Because he's friends with a lot of the so-called
sixth generation film producers, film directors

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:18.900
in China who are now getting systematically
shut down by President Chi and his administration,

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:27.260
the party. He knows what's happening in China
and he's continually impressed that you can

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:34.830
make such anti-government, such anti-American
films in Hollywood and get away with it. That's

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:43.230
the sort of gut feeling I think a lot of people
in authoritarian societies have to these shows.

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:49.440
There was a villain in the second season of
House of Cards. I hope none of you made it

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:55.390
to the second season. It was even worse than
the first. It was a Chinese villain. This

00:37:55.390 --> 00:38:02.100
raised great concern in the Central Department
of Propaganda and Thought Work. I'm not inventing

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:09.100
that name. There were high level meetings
and a writer at the English language China

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:15.680
Daily, and also at People's Daily, the Chinese
language national party newspaper, was commissioned

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:22.040
to write an article explaining that House
of Cards, yes, they had a Chinese villain,

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:28.650
but don't worry the show, and I quote, "Is
actually a strong diatribe against the political

00:38:28.650 --> 00:38:35.500
system in the United States." Everybody felt
better.

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:42.500
I guess I've gone on over my time. I was going
to make some points at the end but I look

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:51.210
at my clock with horror. Wait, it's 1:20?
My watch just picked a funny time to stop.

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:57.610
I'm glad there's another clock up here. I'll
keep going. Six weeks ago I took my Chinese

00:38:57.610 --> 00:39:04.160
visitor to see The Interview, a show that
was in the headlines recently, a potty-mouthed,

00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:12.000
blood-spattered comedy about two hapless American
journalists recruited by the CIA to assassinate

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:20.070
the leader of North Korea, Kim Jong-un, the
dictator of Korea I should say.

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:24.730
Now as you may remember, The Interview was
scheduled to open on Christmas Day. It's release

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:31.500
was cancelled and then limited by Sony Pictures
because of a recent cyber-attack on Sony Pictures

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:38.760
and because of a vague terrorist threat that
was said to emanate from North Korea. Based

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:43.030
on the media coverage of these events, not
to mention President Obama's denunciation

00:39:43.030 --> 00:39:49.670
of Sony for caving into terrorist, my Chinese
Fulbright scholar was expecting a bold and

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:56.490
sophisticated satire, the kind of movie you
can't make in China.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:40:10.650
What he got was one part satire, and not very
good satire, to ten parts rectum jokes, boner

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:17.080
jokes, vomit jokes, diarrhea jokes, death
by poison jokes, death by automatic weapons

00:40:17.080 --> 00:40:23.270
jokes, severed fingers spurting blood jokes,
tank, helicopter and battle jokes and finally

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:30.270
the biggest joke, Kim's face starting to melt
before his body is engulfed in flame. That's

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:38.510
not even mentioning the profanity, which seemed
to genuinely shock my Chinese visitor. I was

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:45.970
really amazed. He couldn't get over the profanity.
On the way home, I reminded him of a comment

00:40:46.240 --> 00:40:51.700
that he'd made when I first spoke with him
in Beijing, which I quoted in my book. He

00:40:51.700 --> 00:41:01.980
said, "I don't like censorship but it does
sanitize the image of American in China."

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:08.090
I don't like censorship either and I do not
advocate government control either of the

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:13.720
entertainment industry or of its exports but,
as I will explain in a minute, I see nothing

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:18.680
wrong with urging Hollywood to be more mindful
of the impact some of its products are having

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:27.720
on foreign perceptions of America.
Part three, what is required for a solution?

00:41:27.730 --> 00:41:33.600
I'm going to set forth two things which I
think will have to be borne in mind in any

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:39.910
effort to improve America's reputation in
the world. They're both based in time-honored

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:47.910
traditions that are unique to America and
instrumental in her greatness in the world.

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:54.170
Now the first is that public diplomacy conducted
by the government and probably public diplomacy,

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:59.200
I don't think I said this before, simply means
the US government addressing the public in

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:03.140
foreign countries. Not the leaders, not the
diplomats, not elites necessarily, although

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:09.600
elites are included, but also the broader
population, that's why it's called public

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:21.550
diplomacy, that it be based on the truth,
even if that means fighting an asymmetrical

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:28.200
war with our adversaries.
The second requirement is that Americans in

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:35.160
general, and Hollywood in particular, remember
that there's a difference between coercive

00:42:35.160 --> 00:42:42.840
state censorship and freely chosen voluntary
restraints on speech by individuals, organizations

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:51.350
and societies. I mean first I'll say quickly
about the public diplomacy part. The propagandists

00:42:51.350 --> 00:43:00.710
of Russia, China, Iran, and ISIS and Al Qaeda
and all those other groups pursue a two-pronged

00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:08.590
strategy. First they go in for what Joseph
Goebbels called The Big Lie, a false historical

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:14.800
narrative that in all these cases portrays
the United States as the leader of an evil

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:21.540
western conspiracy to subjugate the rest of
the world.

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:25.100
Now when they're faced with evidence that
contradicts their big lie or suspects that

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:29.810
somebody might not be buying their big lie,
they go in, particularly Russia is particularly

00:43:29.810 --> 00:43:38.840
good at this, they go in for The Big Confusion,
flooding every available media space with

00:43:38.840 --> 00:43:47.790
bizarre rumors, conspiracy theories, paranoid
fantasies, in the hope that their audiences

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:54.790
will simply give up on caring about what really
happened and become cynical and decide there's

00:43:55.060 --> 00:44:00.610
no real truth to the matter. It's just everything
is an opinion. Everything's propaganda so

00:44:00.610 --> 00:44:04.890
it's all BS and just become very jaded and
cynical.

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:11.800
That's very, very true of Russians and Russian
speakers and Russia's neighbors, neighboring

00:44:11.800 --> 00:44:17.830
countries, and the Putin regime is very skilled
at dealing with these very jaded, cynical

00:44:17.830 --> 00:44:24.110
populations. It's also trying to build up
the historical narrative of the glory and

00:44:24.110 --> 00:44:31.110
wonder of Russia and so forth and rebuild
a kind of idealism. Good luck with that. How

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:38.540
do we respond to this? It's different from
the Soviet Union's Communist ideology which,

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:45.060
of course, itself lost its credence long before
the end of the Cold War.

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.860
How do you deal with people who are so confused
and cynical they don't think there's any such

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:55.860
thing as an objective truth or even a basic
fact about the Malaysian Airline that came

00:44:56.800 --> 00:45:03.800
down? Nobody knows. Who's going to know? It's
all lies. Do we fight fire with fire and bombard

00:45:04.810 --> 00:45:11.810
these populations with counter-propaganda?
This is very tempting to a lot of people,

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:18.260
especially if they haven't been thinking about
it a whole lot. Should we do what comes naturally

00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:27.050
to a society where liberty is sacrosanct?
Where liberty is sacrosanct, gather the facts,

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:32.740
articulate the principles at stake and disseminate
them both as forcefully as possible, even

00:45:32.740 --> 00:45:39.010
if some aspects of the story do not reflect
well on the United States.

00:45:39.010 --> 00:45:44.210
Now there was a time when America got involved
in blatant propaganda, which is to say big

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:52.530
lies. This was before America's entry into
World War I. Perhaps some of you know that

00:45:52.530 --> 00:46:00.370
the British were very anxious to get American
involved in the war. The British government

00:46:00.370 --> 00:46:07.370
disseminated a great deal of hate propaganda
against the Germans in particular. They fabricated

00:46:07.510 --> 00:46:12.970
stories of atrocities committed by the Germans
in Belgium and elsewhere. They were fabrications

00:46:12.970 --> 00:46:18.780
and they were widely disseminated.
Even the fledgling movie industry in the United

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:28.230
States got into the act, making films with
titles like, The Hun and The Kaiser, The Beast

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:36.110
of Berlin. I actually saw part of that once.
It's not a very good movie. After World War

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:42.740
I, by the 1930's, both the British and the
American governments realized how much damage

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:52.200
this had done. In the first place, it inspired
Joseph Goebbels and Adolph Hitler. The Big

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:58.740
Lie, hate propaganda, could be done through
modern media. Some of their inspiration came

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:03.040
from what the British had done before World
War I. Other of course came from what the

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:07.190
Soviets were doing.
What the British and Americans did in this

00:47:07.190 --> 00:47:15.310
one instance was uncomfortably similar to
what the Soviets and the Nazis had done. The

00:47:15.310 --> 00:47:20.650
other damage really bad damage was that when
the first stories began to circulate in Europe

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:27.650
about Nazi atrocities in the 30's, people
didn't believe them because they thought they

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:34.430
had been fabricated. There's a wonderful historian
in Britain who's written a lot about this,

00:47:34.430 --> 00:47:41.430
my main source. This is why during World War
II and the Cold War, Britain and America took

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:48.450
the relatively high road.
There was no lack of spying and lying in both

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:55.140
of those conflicts but when it came to communicating
with large foreign populations, both Britain

00:47:55.140 --> 00:48:01.300
and America refrained from spreading blatant
lies and disinformation. This is when the

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:08.300
word propaganda lost its neutrality and became
a bad word. Compared with the propaganda of

00:48:08.390 --> 00:48:14.100
the Third Reich and the propaganda of the
Soviet Community party, this approach was

00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:20.450
decidedly asymmetrical. You know what? It
worked.

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:30.880
Okay last piece. Popular culture. Requirements
for a solution for our popular culture. The

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:37.240
last chapter of my book is full of practical
recommendations. They're still fresh. No one

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:48.200
has taken me up on them although they actually
are being read by a few people in Washington.

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:54.100
For this last piece, I want to say something
about censorship, going back to what I said

00:48:54.100 --> 00:48:59.530
earlier. This is the last thing I'll say.
Of course just as the story about The Interview

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:03.830
and Sony was fading from the headlines, at
New Year's we had the attacks on the satirical

00:49:03.840 --> 00:49:14.630
weekly Charlie Hebdo in Paris by self-taught
homegrown terrorists and of course on the

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:20.410
kosher supermarket and other targets.
The attack on Charlie Hebdo in particular

00:49:20.410 --> 00:49:29.370
inspired a great outcry supporting an absolute
principle of free speech. According to which

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:37.850
all forms of expression, including obscenity,
slander, blasphemy, are acceptable and all

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:44.310
limits on expression, from coercive censorship
to voluntary restraint by individuals and

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:52.310
society are unacceptable. Now the logic here
is that of the slippery slope. Every limit

00:49:52.330 --> 00:50:00.290
on speech is defined as perforce a fatal step
on the road to tyranny and the destruction

00:50:00.290 --> 00:50:05.330
of freedom. Therefore, no limits on speech
should ever be placed.

00:50:05.330 --> 00:50:10.820
Whilst in the outcry was the fact that this
absolute principle has never been upheld by

00:50:10.820 --> 00:50:16.690
any society, including modern democratic societies.
Indeed, in every society known to humanity,

00:50:16.690 --> 00:50:25.050
there have been limits of some kind on speech.
The real question is why, in spite of this

00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:30.840
fact, some societies remain much freer than
others? Here's another thing that was lost

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:36.040
in that outcry. This is the distinction I'm
highlighting for you, which is the distinction

00:50:36.040 --> 00:50:44.040
between coercive censorship, in particular
state censorship, legal censorship, and voluntary

00:50:44.050 --> 00:50:48.160
restraint.
Now of course some forms of state censorship

00:50:48.160 --> 00:50:54.880
and coercion are softer than others. In China,
for example, and Russia, you can just make

00:50:54.880 --> 00:51:01.630
one professor disappear and the other professors
will get the hint. My Fulbright friend, one

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:07.070
of his colleagues at the Foreign Studies University
was recently demoted to stacking books in

00:51:07.070 --> 00:51:13.470
the library. He taken, his stuff removed from
his office, he's gone. He's not in prison.

00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:19.030
As long as he behaves himself, he won't be
in prison. He's in the basement of the building

00:51:19.030 --> 00:51:23.380
stacking books in the library. This was somebody
who was teaching classes and talking about

00:51:23.380 --> 00:51:32.230
Tiananmen Square and American culture and
film and he's gone. I don't know how my friend

00:51:32.230 --> 00:51:37.740
will react frankly.
This is what people mean usually when they

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:43.550
talk about self-censorship. They mean that
a powerful police state or some other overweening

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:52.230
authority has intimidated you into silence.
This is not the same thing as voluntary restraint

00:51:52.250 --> 00:52:02.240
in a liberal democracy. Here's the difference.
Voluntary restraint is voluntary. When exercised

00:52:02.240 --> 00:52:10.920
by an individual, we call it tact, discretion,
prudence, reticence. When exercised by a society,

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:19.040
we call it custom, propriety, social norms,
public morality.

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:24.510
I think you'll agree with me these days it's
easier to defend individual restraint on speech

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:30.710
than it is to defend social restraints. This
is because social restraints have been roundly

00:52:30.710 --> 00:52:36.980
condemned by such champions of free speech,
such as John Stuart Mill, for example. Indeed,

00:52:36.980 --> 00:52:45.170
a big part of Mill's argument in his famous
book on liberty is against, "The hostile and

00:52:45.170 --> 00:52:54.130
dreaded censorship" of ingrained social attitudes.
From this and many other sources we in the

00:52:54.140 --> 00:53:00.440
west have inherited the idea that freedom
depends on letting creative individuals push

00:53:00.440 --> 00:53:08.470
the envelope of accepted social norms.
Mill's sharpest critic, James FitzJames Stephen

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:17.550
took issue with this view. His reply to Mill
was simple and commonsensical. It's actually

00:53:17.550 --> 00:53:23.610
quite long and complex but he did say some
simple and commonsensical things, one of which

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:30.490
was, "The custom of looking upon certain courses
of conduct with aversion is the essence of

00:53:30.490 --> 00:53:40.270
morality." Okay. Here's the problem as I see
it for us. Mill's assumption is now playing

00:53:40.270 --> 00:53:48.990
out on a global stage where the decisive battle
over free speech is not being fought between

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:56.020
the creative individual and social conformity.
It's being fought between those who would

00:53:56.130 --> 00:54:03.130
defend the fundamental liberties of the west
and those who would destroy them.

00:54:03.180 --> 00:54:08.950
To wage that battle, we in the west must do
more than cheer for every gross-out comedy

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:14.810
and in your face publication that comes along.
We must think seriously about the limits that

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:21.340
we do place on speech and expression, why
we place those limits and, most important,

00:54:21.340 --> 00:54:33.580
how we place those limits. Here I think America
has a valuable lesson to each Europe. Here

00:54:33.580 --> 00:54:43.200
in America, if I paraphrase Will Rogers, "We're
all self-censors, only on different topics."

00:54:43.200 --> 00:54:49.050
Consider the vast majority of Americans now
disapprove of derogatory racial and ethnic

00:54:49.050 --> 00:54:54.050
epithets. The question is, how did this come
about?

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:58.520
For most Americans this particular restraint
on speech reflects a strong social consensus

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:05.520
that developed slowly over time. We don't
consider it self-censorship most of the time,

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:12.680
except sometimes. Those time are when we feel
intimidated or coerced by someone in authority

00:55:12.970 --> 00:55:19.890
not to speak freely. In other words, when
we feel silenced by some imposed rule or law.

00:55:19.890 --> 00:55:26.640
It takes a long time for a given restraint
on speech to take hold, to become accepted,

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:32.070
not just by a particular group, an elite,
but by everybody. Because that process is

00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:40.830
so slow, people are tempted to speed it up
by passing a law or other coercive measure.

00:55:40.840 --> 00:55:48.920
We see this pattern in our American universities
where 40 years ago roughly an informal campaign

00:55:48.930 --> 00:55:58.330
began to purge academic discourse of racial
bias, gender bias, disability bias, and every

00:55:58.330 --> 00:56:03.880
other conceivable form of bias which, as you
may know, have taken truly elaborate forms

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:10.880
these days. Now this campaign began informally.
It went much too slowly for some of its more

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:17.910
ardent proponents. It also suffered a setback
when critics stuck it with the old Stalinist

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:24.910
label of political correctness. I can still
remember Irving Crystal's wife, Gertrude Himmelfarb,

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:31.070
in 1991 the joy with which she discovered
that phrase, political correctness, because

00:56:31.070 --> 00:56:36.530
it captured so much of what she was trying
to criticize about what's going on in academia.

00:56:36.530 --> 00:56:42.530
Believe me, that was setback for the politically
correct.

00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:47.680
The reaction was predictable and completely
counterproductive. That would be of course

00:56:47.680 --> 00:56:54.020
the university speech codes that we now have,
that have been imposed on institutions whose

00:56:54.020 --> 00:57:02.580
entire raison d'etre is free inquiry. Now
on a larger scale, you can see the same pattern

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:09.980
in Europe where many countries have so-called
hate speech laws on the books. For example,

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:16.620
in France, where the civil law tradition is
more proscriptive than the Anglo-American

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:23.530
common law tradition, and France also has
no 1st Amendment as you know.

00:57:23.530 --> 00:57:30.530
In 1990, the French National Assembly passed
the Gayssot Act, which is one of several Holocaust

00:57:31.090 --> 00:57:38.090
denial laws on the books in Europe and of
course also in Israel. A few years later,

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:44.540
France's fundamental press law, which was
passed in 1881, was amended to prohibit hate

00:57:44.540 --> 00:57:50.760
speech based on race, religion, gender, and
sexual orientation. Sounds kind of like an

00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:57.630
American university, doesn't it? This has
led to many prosecutions of individuals and

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:06.870
publications whose stock in trade is outrageous
speech. One such is Dieudonné, a child of

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:12.950
the Bonheur outside Paris who went from being
a popular comedian working with a Jewish boyhood

00:58:12.950 --> 00:58:20.870
friend to being France's most notorious anti-Semite
and Holocaust denier. Another target of these

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:27.870
laws has been the satirical weekly Charlie
Hebdo, which never tires of attacking religion.

00:58:28.340 --> 00:58:36.060
Just to cite one example, in 2011, it ran
a cover showing 3 rolls of toilet paper; one

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:43.760
labeled Torah, one labeled Bible, and one
labeled Koran, under the caption, "In the

00:58:43.760 --> 00:58:52.280
toilet all the religions." Another cover shows
a rabbi and an SS officer sharing a sloppy

00:58:52.280 --> 00:58:58.920
kiss outside the gates of Auschwitz. These
are mild compared to what you find inside

00:58:58.920 --> 00:59:07.590
Charlie Hebdo. For example, one image that
was sent to me by a friend in Germany actually

00:59:07.590 --> 00:59:14.590
whose brother collects Charlie Hebdos was
an image of Christ committing an act of sodomy

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:21.950
with God. Clearly, Charlie Hebdo is an equal
opportunity offender. However, during the

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:27.990
last decade it has lavished particular venom
on Islam.

00:59:27.990 --> 00:59:34.680
In 2013, it ran a two-part series called The
Life Of Mohammed, in which Mohammed is depicted

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:44.000
as a fat, ugly, lecher with a bad case of
priapism displayed in graphic X rated detail.

00:59:44.020 --> 00:59:50.420
Now some of the lawsuits brought against Dieudonné
and Charlie Hebdo have won. Others have lost,

00:59:50.420 --> 00:59:58.650
forcing the offenders to pay steep fines and
lawyer's fees. All have been decided on fine

00:59:58.650 --> 01:00:03.780
points of law, which make little sense to
the public.

01:00:03.780 --> 01:00:10.500
For example, France has a law against hate
speech. I know it's after lunch but stay with

01:00:10.500 --> 01:00:16.700
me on this. This is a fine point of the law.
France has a law against hate speech based

01:00:16.700 --> 01:00:25.970
on religion, but no law against blasphemy.
This means you can trash a fellow citizen

01:00:25.970 --> 01:00:34.770
whose beliefs you abhor, no wait, you cannot
trash a fellow citizen whose beliefs you abhor

01:00:34.780 --> 01:00:40.640
but you can trash his beliefs.
Now I don't know about you. I'm not a lawyer,

01:00:40.640 --> 01:00:47.640
but that looks to me a lot like a hair waiting
to be split. That is precisely the problem

01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:53.480
when lawyers and judges and courts regulate
speech, the line between sophistication and

01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:59.660
sophistry becomes blurred. The result is,
to judge by what's going on in Europe right

01:00:59.660 --> 01:01:06.660
now, is ever uglier and more provocative violations
of the law.

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:12.770
I would close very briefly by contrasting
that with the policy of US newspapers in the

01:01:12.770 --> 01:01:18.860
wake of the Danish cartoons and also the Charlie
Hebdo, not to reprint some of these images,

01:01:18.860 --> 01:01:25.630
for which they were excoriated. There's actually
a blasphemy law on the books in Massachusetts

01:01:25.630 --> 01:01:32.530
from 1697. If the Boston Globe ran one of
these cartoons and they got prosecuted under

01:01:32.530 --> 01:01:36.490
that law, the case would get thrown out of
court First Amendment grounds, subsequent

01:01:36.490 --> 01:01:40.220
court cases, there's no way you could make
that stick.

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:44.150
That's not why the Globe and other newspapers
in the United States didn't publish those

01:01:44.150 --> 01:01:49.170
cartoons. They didn't publish them because
they have a tradition of not publishing material

01:01:49.170 --> 01:01:56.040
that is grossly offensive to people's religious
sensibilities. Voluntary restraint. It's a

01:01:56.040 --> 01:02:04.070
long-standing custom for which they have been
excoriated for not defending free speech by

01:02:04.070 --> 01:02:14.070
publishing some of these cartoons.
I would just close by saying this because

01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:24.390
I'm getting a few signals from some of my
supporters here. Something like this, I think.

01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:29.790
The world's most robust tradition of free
speech and expression does not consist of

01:02:29.790 --> 01:02:35.440
a blanket refusal to set any limits. Rather
it consists of a preference for voluntary

01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:42.119
restraint, both individual and communal, over
coercive censorship, especially by the state.

01:02:42.119 --> 01:02:47.590
Much as Americans love liberty, we must not
let that love blind us to the importance of

01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:50.670
occasionally holding our tongue. Thank you.

